<p>There's a simple way in bash to add commas between all array elements:</p>
<div class="highlight"><pre><span></span><code><span class="nv">fruits</span><span class="o">=(</span>apple<span class="w"> </span>orange<span class="w"> </span>apple<span class="o">)</span>
<span class="nv">IFS</span><span class="o">=</span>,<span class="w"> </span><span class="nb">echo</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="s2">"</span><span class="si">${</span><span class="nv">fruits</span><span class="p">[*]</span><span class="si">}</span><span class="s2">"</span>
</code></pre></div>

<p>This will output:</p>
<div class="highlight"><pre><span></span><code>apple,orange,apple
</code></pre></div>

<p>Cool, right? It turns out, the array-to-string expression,
<code>${fruits[*]}</code>, will use the first value of <code>IFS</code> as the
separator. That's why the default behaviour of <code>"${fruits[*]}"</code> will
be a space separated list, because the first character in default
<code>IFS</code> is space.</p>
<p><code>IFS</code> in front of the (echo) command makes it local to only that
command execution.</p>
<p>Code tested on:</p>
<div class="highlight"><pre><span></span><code>$ bash --version
GNU bash, version 5.3.15(1)-release (aarch64-apple-darwin25.4.0)
</code></pre></div>

